{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Yu2014-Optik-stack17-HMLM-mirrors",
  "Source": "K. Yu, J. Yin, D. Huang, and J. Bao, \"Design of non-polarization angle-tuned narrowband thin film filter,\" Optik 125, 397-400 (2014). doi:10.1016/j.ijleo.2013.06.089 - Eq. (17), p. 399 (Sec. 3); H = Si, M = Ta2O5, L = SiO2 from Sec. 2.2 p. 398",
  "Notes": "Four-cavity filter with three-material (HMLM)^m mirrors chosen so Rs = Rp at 25 deg (equal s/p bandwidths), still with a 4L spacer (s/p centres still split)\nSpecification: Equal s and p bandwidths at 25 deg (mirror condition Rp = Rs, i.e. W = 1 with U real, Eqs. 11-15, U = 30, m = 8).\nReported: Fig. 3 (25 deg; the text on p. 399 says \"stack (18)\" but the caption says stack (17)): s and p passbands of equal width with centres separated, s ~1519.4 nm and p ~1519.8 nm (read from the plot, axis 1518-1521 nm). Mirror design point: n_M = 2.05, n_H = 3.86 for n_L = 1.465 and U = 30 (fourth intersection in Fig. 2).\nNotes: \"H4LH\" = H, 4L, H. 255 layers as printed, no adjacent same-material layers, so 255 layers (267 quarter waves). No printed total. The stack is a palindrome, so which side faces the light does not change the layer order. Incident medium taken as air (n0 in Eqs. 4-15; the paper does not name it) and the 25 deg angle as in air. Substrate glass n_G = 1.523 (p. 397) given as an inline constant. The high index H is \"Si\" with n_H = 3.86, the value the U/W analysis requires (p. 398); the paper says it then picked Si from a database, so its simulation may have used real Si (~3.48 at 1.55 um) instead of 3.86 - the main uncertainty of this rebuild. Si treated as non-absorbing at 1.5 um.\nLayers from Eq. (17), p. 399 (Sec. 3); H = Si, M = Ta2O5, L = SiO2 from Sec. 2.2 p. 398; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours at 25 deg s 1519.66, p 1519.78 nm; paper s ~1519.4, p ~1519.8 nm (Fig. 3). Uses the paper’s Si n = 3.86 (real Si is about 3.48 at 1.55 um).",
  "Wavelength": [
    1567
  ],
  "Materials": [
    {
      "Name": "Si n=3.86 (Yu 2014 Optik)",
